A||||4||DJRice||any scanner/digital imaging gurus in the house?||||5||
Z||000000||DJRice||01-16-2001||08:55 PM||||I have been thinking about buying a scanner and I have a question about "maximum interpolated resolution".  <P>If two scanners both have an optical resolution of 600x1200 dpi yet one has a 2400dpi max interpolated res and the other has a 9600 dpi max interpolated res, which one is the better scanner as far as picture  quality.  Is this difference due to the software packaged with the unit?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>Advice is appreciated!<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick's Dad||01-16-2001||11:26 PM||||(WARNING! Fairly lengthy response ahead....)<P>They are, essentially, the same quality scanner, until you hit the guesswork.<P>"Optical resolution" is what the scanner actually sees as it passes the image and/or document. It scans at 600x1200 dpi, unless you've instructed it to scan at a lower rate. This, officially, is the best it can do.<P>Interpolation is the guesswork part of the equasion. Essentially, the computer (guided by the packaged software drivers), "looks" at two adjacent dots, and "sees" that one is red, and the next one is red, then there could be one or two in between that are also red. Sometimes, one is red and one is blue, so the one(s) in between might be violet. Or mauve. Or peuce. It's hard to say, without seeing the real thing, but the computer will try it, anyway.<P>One other important thing to consider is the construction of the unit. Make sure you have a good defective exchange policy where you buy your scanner. If and when you take one home, set it up and scan a piece of paper that you've taken a ruler to and drawn a diagonal line. Scan at the highest optical resolution only. Take that image, and enlarge it on the screen until you can see the individual pixels. The diagonal should not only be jaggy, but it should be consistently jaggy. If it isn't, then the scanner may have been excessively punished by the shipping and warehousing facets of its jurney to you. Depending on the unit, however, this could also be poor production tolerances. Be careful with whom you deal. A local store with a good service policy should be the one to get your $$.<P>Hope this helps....<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||01-17-2001||08:46 AM||||He's right on! Don't let them fool you with all that techno babble. Interpolation is a software function and actually has nothing to do with the scanner.<P>I was a graphic designer for 8 years for a Fortune 200 company before I went into the music business full time. I had a huge budget that I could buy all the goodies I wanted. Always had the newest toys to play with.... And I can tell you that my $79 scanner that I use now at home is just as good as anything I had then. <P>Look for TWAIN compliance so you can use Photoshop to scan your images. Look for OCR if that is important to you. I never use OCR because there are too many chances for mistakes that a spell check will not catch. <P>Interface can also be important. If you don't have room to add another card in your system, you might want the kind that hooks to your parallel port, or maybe USB if your computer is equipped. I never was a fan of the parallel scanners that hook between your computer and printer. In the old days, they seemed to mess with the bisynchronous signal. My $79 baby is parallel though and has been working fine for a year and a half with no interference.<P>The bottom line is the same as always. Decide what you need now, and what you realistically think you will need in the near future. Don't fall for the marketing hype and think you have to have the 'newest and best' because by the time you get it hooked up there will be something 'newer and better'! I know! I did the marketing for years! heheh Not in computers but it all works the same...<P>Good luck...<p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 01-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||01-17-2001||02:17 PM||||What's also important is that it's actually able to scan at <B>lower</B> resolutions. This might sound strange, but most scanners nowadays have a fixed resolution at which they scan. Any "lower" settings are done by a combination of software and hardware and the results are far from satisfactory.<P>Let me try to explain this. Suppose a scanner has a horizontal resolution of 300dpi. This means that it is able to scan 300 points for every inch. Each "scan-point" starts where the previous ended. Now we want to scan something at 150dpi. Good scanners will use larger scan-points so that even at this lower resolution scan-points are still adjacent. <I>Bad</I> scanners use the same size scan-points, but use fewer of them to get to the lower resolution:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scan1.gif"> <P>Now as you can see, the 150dpi scan with the 300dpi scan points is <B>not</B> scanning everything. The true 300dpi scan scans everything. Now this doesn't seem like a problem... until you scan a <B>halftoned</B> image. Halftoning is a technique where the full scale of grayscales or colors are emulated by using only a few base-colors. Every single picture in a magazine is printed this way. Only 4 base-colors are used: yellow, cyan, red and black (and sometimes an extra color: magenta). If you take a magnifying glass and look at a picture in a magazine, you'll see exactly what I mean. Grayscale halftoning uses only one color: black. For instance, 50% black (grey) is emulated by alternating a black pixel with white space. Now what happens if you scan a halftoned image? Here's the 50% black example:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scan2.gif"> <P>As you can see, the true 150dpi scanned image will result in gray, because it averages the color within a single scanpoint. The 150dpi scan with the 300dpi scan-points will result in.. a black image!<P>Usually, the difference between the two scanners is what is used for scan-optics. The good scanners use a system of lenses with a CCD chip (almost like a camera). The bad scanners use a bar with a fixed number of optical receptors in a row.<P>To find out what kind of scanner you are about to buy, I suggest you try to scan a picture out of a magazine when you are in the shop. Scan it at low resolution and the result will tell you immediately what kind of scanner it is.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||01-17-2001||04:58 PM||||Good point, Cliff... <P>And that is very important for doing web images too. As I mentioned in another post last week, all you need for the web is 72dpi. That is where some people make mistakes and it takes forever for their webpages to load.<P>I love the new Photoshop that has ImageReady built into it. I used to have to do all the changes manually to make my images optimized for the web. Now the program takes care of it for me...||||reg||1
